div.p:
XII
You have a name that names the season of the tender year,
when the Cecropian bees plunder the brief spring;
a name that deserved to be painted with Acidalia's reed,
that Cytherea delights to write with her own needle;
a name that, made a letter with Erythraean pearls,
a gem polished by the Heliads' thumb might mark;
that the cranes might raise to the stars with writing wing;
that befits being only in Caesar's house.

XII
You promise everything when you've drunk all night;
in the morning you provide nothing. Pollio, drink in the morning.

XIII
If autumn gave me my name, I'd be Oporinos;
if the harsh stars of winter, Chimerinos;
named from the summer month, I'd be called Therinos by you:
but he to whom the spring season gave his name — who is he?

XVII. The Gaming-Board
On this side the counter is scored by twice-six pips;
On this the discolored piece dies to a twin foe.

XIX. Nuts
Gambling with nuts seems small and harmless;
Yet often it has stripped boys of their backsides.

XXVII. A Branch of Date-Palms
The golden date is held out on Janus' Kalends;
Yet this is wont to be a poor man's gift.

XXXVI. Barber's Tools
These tools are fit for trimming your hair;
This one's good for long nails, that one for cheeks.

XXXVIII. A Bundle of Reed-Pens
The Memphian land gives reeds handy for paper;
Let the rest of the marsh be woven into roofing for you.

XXXIX
If there is anyone to be numbered among rare friends,
Of the kind that old fidelity and ancient fame have known;
If anyone is steeped in the arts of Cecropian and Latin Minerva,
And good with a true plainness of heart;
If anyone is a guardian of the right, an admirer of the honourable,
And one who asks the gods nothing with a secret mouth;
If anyone is upheld by the strength of a great mind:
May I perish if that man is not Decianus.

XLVII
The things that make life happier,
most delightful Martial, are these:
wealth not earned by toil, but inherited;
land not ungrateful, a hearth ever burning;
no lawsuit, the toga seldom worn, a quiet mind;
a freeman's strength, a healthy body;
prudent frankness, well-matched friends;
easy company, a table without art;
a night not drunken, but freed of cares;
a bed not joyless, and yet chaste;
sleep that makes the darkness brief;
to wish to be what you are, to prefer nothing else;
neither to fear your last day nor to long for it.

LIV
Every morning you tell me sheer dreams about myself,
to stir and trouble my mind.
The last vintage has gone to the dregs, and this one too,
while the witch wears out my nights to placate you;
I have used up the salted meal and the heaps of incense;
my flocks have dwindled, while lamb after lamb falls;
no pig, no farmyard birds, no eggs are left.
Either keep watch, Nasidianus, or dream for yourself.

LXXI
Four pounds of silver you sent me at winter time,
Postumianus, ten years ago,
to one expecting more — for gifts ought to stand or grow —
two came, more or less;
the third and fourth brought much less;
in the fifth it was a Septician pound indeed;
in the sixth year we came down to an eight-ounce dish;
after this a half-pound, scraped, in a small cup, was given;
the eighth sent a spoon weighing less than two ounces;
the ninth scarcely brought a little ladle lighter than a needle.
Now the tenth year has nothing left to send us:
go back, Postumianus, to your four pounds.

LXXV
Polytimus hastens to the girls;
unwilling, Hypnus confesses himself a boy;
Secundus has buttocks fed on acorns;
Dindymus is soft, but doesn't want to be;
Amphion could have been born a girl.
The charms and the pride of these
and their querulous airs, Avitus, I'd rather have
than five times two hundred thousand as a dowry.

LXXIX. Live Mullets
The mullet breathes in the brought-in sea, but is sluggish now;
It grows faint. Give it live water: it will be strong.

LXXXVII
If my Flaccus delights in a long-eared fox-ape,
if Canius enjoys a grim Ethiopian;
if Publius burns with love for a tiny lapdog,
if Cronius loves a monkey like himself;
if a destructive ichneumon pleases Marius,
if a chattering magpie pleases you, Lausus;
if Cadilla winds a cold serpent round her neck,
if Telesilla gave a tomb to a nightingale:
why should not the man who sees such monsters please their owners
love the charming face of Cupid-like Labyrtas?

LXXXVIII
You chase after all the praetors' little books, Cotta;
you receive their wax tablets too. You're a dutiful man.

XCIX. A Basket
I, a basket, came barbarian from the painted Britons;
But now Rome would rather call me her own.

CLV. White Wools
Apulia is noble for first-rate fleeces, Parma for the second;
The Altinum sheep wins praise for the third.

CLXXXVII. Menander's Thaïs
With this play he first toyed with the wanton loves of youth;
Glycera was no boy's mistress — Thaïs was.

CCXXI. A Grill with Spits
Let the thin tidbit sweat for you on the curved grill;
Let the foaming boar steam on the long spit.
